    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Agility Bandits began as an agility-focused social identity and later
        expanded into a complete blog brand. The visual system needed to keep
        the personality of Roi, my red-and-white Border Collie, while expressing
        the speed, unpredictability, and humour of dog agility across profile
        images, navigation, page graphics, and larger layouts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The identity had to feel playful and personal without losing
            legibility. A detailed dog portrait, a bandit theme, and a bold
            display wordmark all needed to remain balanced when the mark was
            reduced for social or navigation use.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Capture Roi's recognisable colouring and expression</li>
            <li>Make the bandit concept immediate without overpowering the dog</li>
            <li>Create energy that could extend beyond the primary logo</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/logo-agilitybandits-600.webp"
            data-src="logo-agilitybandits-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="473"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Agility Bandits logo with a red-and-white Border Collie wearing a bandit mask above a Western-style wordmark"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Character-led primary logo featuring
            Roi and the bandit wordmark</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Vectorised Roi with simplified fur shapes and natural colours so
              the character remains personal and readable
            </li>
            <li>
              Added a clear eye mask and Western-style display type to connect
              the dog portrait with the “Bandits” name
            </li>
            <li>
              Built full, horizontal, and icon-focused options so the identity
              can move between social, web, and print placements
            </li>
            <li>
              Limited the wider brand palette to greens and dark neutrals, using
              Roi's fur as the main contrasting colour
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp"
            data-src="design-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="441"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Agility Bandits vector graphics showing speed lines and falling dog agility obstacles"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Motion-inspired obstacle graphics
            communicate speed and chaotic fun</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block">
          <h2>From mark to system</h2>
          <p>
            Speed lines and tipping obstacles extend the brand's personality
            beyond the logo. Repeating the same angles, stroke weight, and green
            accents creates a graphic language that can frame content without
            competing with it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Used directional lines to suggest acceleration</li>
            <li>Turned agility equipment into recognisable decorative assets</li>
            <li>Applied the elements across header, footer, and page layouts</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/graphics-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp"
            data-src="graphics-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="453"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Agility Bandits logo and obstacle graphics applied to the website header and footer"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Logo and supporting graphics applied
            across the website</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished system gives Agility Bandits a distinct voice that works
        from a compact social icon to a full editorial layout. The portrait
        makes the brand personal, while the motion-inspired elements create
        enough variety to support the website without relying on the logo
        everywhere. This project strengthened how I build a visual identity from
        one central character and extend it into a flexible set of supporting
        assets.
      </p>
    </div>

